description:
Lay the pure-domain groundwork for the video cutter, mirroring the existing video lib style (module-level functions + Schema types, NO @ffmpeg/* imports in these files).

1. src/lib/video/errors.ts — add a new tagged error alongside the existing ones:
   export class VideoTrimError extends Schema.TaggedError<VideoTrimError>()(
     "VideoTrimError", { fileName: Schema.String, message: Schema.String }) {}
   (Distinct from VideoEncodeError — the cutter never re-encodes, so a generic encode error would be misleading. Effect best-practice: explicit error per failure reason.)

2. src/lib/video/limits.ts — add cutter-specific caps + checker. Stream copy loads the whole file into ffmpeg.wasm MEMFS (memory ~= input + output), so the ceiling is far more generous than the re-encoding converter's CAPS:
     export const CUTTER_CAPS: Record<DeviceClass, {warnBytes;hardBytes}> = {
       desktop: { warnBytes: 500*MB, hardBytes: 1024*MB },
       mobile:  { warnBytes: 200*MB, hardBytes: 500*MB },
     };
   Reuse classifyDevice + SizeVerdict. Either generalize checkFileSize to accept a caps table (default = existing CAPS) or add checkCutterFileSize(bytes, device). Do NOT touch the converter's CAPS / PROCESS_TIME_* (cutter has no process-time dialog — trim is near-instant).

DESIGN: keep both files pure & unit-testable (no DOM/global reads). Every error a Schema.TaggedError with a message field — no plain Error, no untyped throw.
